 whereas Revit does not have that  capability at the current time to my  knowledge so what are the pros and cons  here let's start with Revit Revit is in  extreme demand in the industry and more  and more firms are taking up Revit  you're gonna need a no effort to get  into most of the larger firms most of  the engineers are probably going to be  on Revit so you're gonna have the  ability to document that little bit  better against that you can however  there are some cons as well to Revit for  example once your files get too big and  you start to try and share them with  masses and masses of people the file  becomes unstable pretty quickly and  crashes quite often it also isn't  multi-thread compatible so you're gonna  need a very fast CPU and a dedicated  custom-built PC to run this program with  very large files effectively you're  probably not going to be able to get  away with your off-the-shelf HP computer  that has a  and nothing inside it you're gonna need  at least 16 gigs around a very good CPU  very good GPU to run most of the  projects you need and the other downside  is it is Windows only compatible if you  want to run it on a Mac you're gonna  have to run parallel so what about  ArchiCAD ArchiCAD has some great pros  first of all it's available on Mac and  Windows so you can use it on any  platform and jump between them in your  office if you need to you have the added  benefit of BIM X for free and twin  motion and Unreal Engine playing  catch-up that's a pro slash con for sure  in my personal opinion it has a better  UI and a much easier and faster learning  curve unfortunately though there isn't  as many plugins for archicad and as i've  said before it's starting to play catch  up against Revit now it's catching up  and I think it was surpass it very soon  because it used to be the opposite about  ten years ago where ArchiCAD was the  dominant one and Revit was playing  catch-up but now they seem to be  swapping courses with this new  visualization 